<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gustav Mahler</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1860">1860</a>–<a href="/years/1911">1911</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mahler</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gustav Mahler was an Austrian composer and conductor, known for his large-scale symphonies and song cycles. He served as director of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> Court Opera. He is counted among the figures of the cultural flowering of Vienna under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gustav-mahler.jpg" alt="Gustav Mahler" width="320" height="382" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Moritz Nähr · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Photo_of_Gustav_Mahler_by_Moritz_N%C3%A4hr_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Mahler was born on 7 July 1860 in the village of Kaliště in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, the second son of a German-speaking Jewish family of humble circumstances. His father, Bernhard Mahler, rose from a pedlar's family to become a coachman and then an innkeeper, and married Marie Herrmann in 1857. They had 14 children. In December 1860 the family moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihlava">Jihlava</a>, where Bernhard built up a distillery and tavern business and where Gustav heard street songs, dance tunes, folk melodies and the trumpet calls of the local military band. These sounds later fed his musical vocabulary. He studied at the Vienna Conservatory from 1875 to 1878.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Mahler's first professional conducting job, in the summer of 1880, was an operetta season at the spa town of Bad Hall. Posts followed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ljubljana">Laibach</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, each larger than the last. In Leipzig he took charge of most of Wagner's Ring cycle when a senior colleague fell ill, and scored a public success, though the orchestra resented his dictatorial manner and heavy rehearsals. In 1897 he became director of the Vienna Court Opera, having converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> to secure the post. He also led the Vienna Philharmonic's subscription concerts from 1898 to 1901, and the anti-Semitic press attacked him throughout his ten years in the city.</p><p>Mahler made his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> debut at the Metropolitan Opera on 1 January 1908, conducting Wagner's Tristan und Isolde, and later led the New York Philharmonic. He gave his last concert at Carnegie Hall on 21 February 1911 with a fever. Diagnosed with bacterial endocarditis, he was taken back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and died in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> sanatorium on 18 May 1911, aged 50.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Composing was for most of his life a part-time activity, done in summer studios while conducting paid his living. Apart from juvenilia he wrote only songs and symphonies, and the two forms are closely linked: melodies of the song cycle Lieder eines fahrenden Gesellen appear in the First Symphony, and Wunderhorn songs form movements of the Second, Third and Fourth. He told Sibelius that the symphony must be like the world and embrace everything, and his music mixes bird calls, bugle fanfares and street tunes with solemn matter. He left Das Lied von der Erde unnumbered, hoping to escape the curse of the Ninth Symphony.</p><p>His symphonies were often controversial when first performed. By his death there had been more than 260 performances of them. Nazi-era bans kept the music from much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, but after 1945 a new generation rediscovered it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnold-schoenberg">Arnold Schoenberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alban-berg">Alban Berg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-webern">Anton Webern</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-viennese-school">Second Viennese School</a> were among his early admirers. The International Gustav Mahler Society was founded in 1955.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Gustav Mahler” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Vienna's fin-de-siècle culture rested on this prosperity: the Ringstrasse, the Secession movement, coffee-house intellectual life and the professional class that produced Sigmund Freud, <b>Gustav Mahler</b>, Gustav Klimt and Theodor Herzl all belonged to a prosperous, cosmopolitan urban economy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1898 <b>Gustav Mahler</b> set the Wunderhorn text for voice and orchestra.</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-gedanken-sind-frei">Die Gedanken sind frei</a></li><li><blockquote>Liszt, Saint-Saëns, <b>Mahler</b>, Mussorgsky, Tchaikovsky and Rachmaninoff, in more than a dozen works, quoted it, and it has been used in many film scores and popular works, from the score for Metropolis (1927) and Citizen Kane (1941) to The Shining (1980) and The Lion King.</blockquote><a href="/songs/dies-irae">Dies irae</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Gustav Mahler</b> used it, in a minor key and under its German name &quot;Bruder Martin&quot;, in the third movement of his Symphony No.</blockquote><a href="/songs/frere-jacques">Frère Jacques</a></li><li><blockquote>…tune for four centuries: Jehan Titelouze (1623), Marc-Antoine Charpentier (five settings), Johann Pachelbel, Nicolas de Grigny, Johann Sebastian Bach (the chorale BWV 370 and the organ preludes BWV 631 and 667), Berlioz, Franck, Bruckner, <b>Gustav Mahler</b>, who set the Latin text in Part I of his Symphony No.</blockquote><a href="/songs/veni-creator-spiritus">Veni Creator Spiritus</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Kaliště, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.59,15.3&amp;year=1893" aria-label="Kaliště on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Gustav Mahler"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4194 5795h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4194 5795h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.59,15.3&amp;year=1893">What was here in 1893? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
